of homes might have as many as a  hundred wi-fi devices  in them now i'm not guaranteeing that  that kind of growth rate will  continue last year was a somewhat  unusual year with kovit and everything  but it does indicate that the number of  wi-fi devices in people's homes is  growing very rapidly  and could become a real issue for wi-fi  networks  now the way ofdma works is that it  allows an access point to put together a  single transmission which contains a  small amount of information for each of  many receivers  by combining what would have been many  transmissions into a single packet  a large amount of wasted air time is  saved however  more and more homes are getting multiple  access points to ensure good coverage  and high data rates throughout the home  it turns out this presence of multiple  aps can make it difficult  for ofdma to do its job now here there's  an example from here imagine a home with  eight  iot devices in this example and four  access points  if the iot devices are kind of  distributed among all the aps in the  home  there might be only two devices per ap  so what this means is that each ap can  only aggregate two devices  into their ofdma transmissions that  really limits the amount of benefit that  you might be able to get from ofdma  a potential fix would be to have only  one  ap do this ofdma function  and move all of the iot clients to that  access point  the problem with this approach you can  kind of intuit from the way this is  drawn  is that some devices might be too far  from the access point requiring  transmission at a very low data rate  again destroying any potential  efficiency gain you know when devices  get out to the edge of range in a wi-fi  system they start transmitting at a low  data rate they take a lot of air time  the whole system becomes inefficient  once again so  for each home you know kind of like as i  i like it at the store of goldilocks and  the three bears  for each home there is a kind of just  right solution  that balances this aggregation desire  to get a lot of devices on single access  points so fdma can be effective  with the ability to connect to nearby  access points where your signal strength  is the strongest and where you can go at  the highest data rate  so the correct topology for each home  will be different depending on the  clients the traffic patterns  the layout of the home and the access  points in the home  and so again what's really required is  an intelligent system that optimizes the  network  and sets it up individually and in a  different way for each individual home  so wi-fi six brings two features that  try to manage air time  for advantage target wake time again  this saves power by reserving time slots  for sleeking devices to wake and  communicate  and network slicing to reserve time and  frequency slots for high priority data  flows  as with other features making these work  effectively in a multi-ap environment is  especially tricky so there's a diagram  here that shows  a home that might have three access  points in it and a bunch of clients  the clients distributed among the access  points in the home  looking at this if we think about how  this twt  or target wake time reservation of time  slots might go  ap-1 might reserve a twt sine slot  for phone p1 for example in this diagram  however ap2 in home might reserve an  overlapping  twt slot for p2 so now what happens is  the two awakening phones  which might in practice be fairly close  to each other in the home  they wake up and they'll interfere with  each other during their twt slots  either they transmit at the same time  and collide or they have to wait for  each other to get done  that defeats the entire purpose of  having the twt  protocol the right thing to do is kind  of complicate it for p1 it's important  that apu2  reserve that twt slot even though none  of its clients will use it so  on ap2 it's like hey i'm going to  reserve this slot but i'm going to  reserve it for none of my clients  to allow the right things to happen in  ap1 on the other hand  ap3 might be far enough away that does  not need to reserve that slot  finally the twt slots at ap2 probably  have to be reserved at both 81 and 83.  the possible scenarios for this as you  can imagine are nearly infinite and  depend  again specifically on what's going on in  each individual particular home  now similarly network slicing where we  reserve slots for high  priority traffic needs to be coordinated  across multiple aps as well  and network slicing has the added  complication that some high priority  traffic must  traverse multiple hop so it's got to go  all the way across the network from end  to end  and that involves pops in the backbone  uh or the backhaul of the network as  well as hops out to the individual  client  and timeout needs to be allocated for  the high priority traffic flow  has to be reserved on each hop  considering whether that hop is on the  same frequency channel  as another hop in the home or whether  it's on a different frequency channel  the scenarios shown here are for a  multi-ap home but similar scenarios can  exist in single ap homes that have  interference from neighbors  or interference from other units within  an mdu  and so coordination across multiple aps  both in homes and even between homes  is again particularly beneficial for  wi-fi six if you really want to get  benefit out of these  new technologies in wi-fi six and the  improvements that come from them  you really have to be doing this  sophisticated kinds of optimization  so wi-fi six is a big improvement you  know as much as we're talking about  the complications of it and the  difficulties of using all these new  features  it is a big improvement and service  providers should be excited about  deploying it  the question becomes you know how do you  get the technology particularly in a way  that enables  centralized management that it really  needs  it turns out there is a convenient way  and ronan talked about this as well  opensync is an open sourced software  agent  that can be put on an access point and  it enables that ap  to be monitored and controlled by the  cloud  opensync includes a software-defined  networking capability  which allows it not only to manage wi-fi  but to implement other services as well  services such as cyber security parental  controls  and even wi-fi motion detection again  ronan was talking about  some of the ways that service providers  can make up for  lost arpu as certain services are kind  of drifting away  such as video services  and he mentioned you know being able to  rent new types of equipment and more  sophisticated  systems like wi-fi it's also possible to  bring  new services into the home that  consumers are very willing to pay for  that are different services and maybe  had been previously supplied by service  providers such as cyber security  parental controls  motion detection can be used to create a  physical alarm system it can alert me  if i'm out of the house when there's  motion happening  it also can be used as a wellness  monitoring system i might have elderly  parents that i want to know  got up in the morning and made it to the  kitchen and had breakfast  now this open sink uh turns out that it  has grown  tremendously in popularity it's  supported by all the major chipset  manufacturers  it comes pre-integrated in aps from a  wide variety of vendors and tran is one  of those  and it has been adopted by leading  service providers  both large and small and you know now it  supports wi-fi  six one thing that's nice about open  sync is that if you  put it into your system it runs equally  well on wi-fi five even  going back to what was uh nominally  wi-fi four  um and wi-fi 6 and you could have even  had mixed environments  where you have some wi-fi 6 devices and  some wi-fi  5 devices and it operates very well on  those devices as well  with the current silicon shortage there  can be supply  issues and if you're trying to roll out  wi-fi six to all your customers  can be the case that that becomes a  little bit challenging to do in the  immediate future  that's another advantage of opensync is  that you can deploy  opensync this software technology and  still allow you to make a lot of forward  progress  even while you are still having wi-fi  five  or previous generations available in a  lot of your homes  as you're rolling out wi-fi six  throughout your network  thank you so i do think that wi-fi six  is a great exciting opportunity  and i think that everyone should be  looking forward to that and making plans  to deploy that  but you have to tend to other aspects of  your network as well and make sure you  have a good wi-fi management system in  place  that can benefit all of your wi-fi  systems thank you  thank you bill uh and thank you ronan we  now move to  and i'm gonna  share my screen in a second but  while i'm i'm doing this we already have  some question  coming in and i will immediately move to  the  to the first one um  interesting one um if you were to  it comes from i think uh rebecca and  she's asking us  if you had to pick one what is the  biggest challenge customer  are expecting wi-fi six to solve  i guess i think you address this one  i was on a panel at one time where they  actually had a survey  question about that and the highest  response was actually  interference congestion problems um  and so i thought that was interesting  and then the second was uh the handling  of a large number of devices in the home  and i think that those answers are are  pretty perceptive in a way  you know usually when a new wi-fi  technology rolls out there's a lot of  focus on the higher data rates and so  forth and that part of wi-fi six i think  is important but i do think that it's  more that interference  and handling lots of clients which is  you know the unique  aspect of wi-fi six i think also as well  there's probably a lot of the basics  that are not well implemented in a lot  of the existing wi-fi for  wi-fi five solutions that are out there  today that people will kind of  anticipate wi-fi five is  or sorry wi-fi six is going to fix  simple things like hand over between aps  in a multi-ap home when not done  properly can result in a really  frustrating and very poor experiences  people get stickiness as they move  around their home etc and  i think a lot of them kind of look  towards wi-fi six as being the solution  towards that when in reality  is not necessarily wi-fi standard  implementation it's going to help with  that it's more a better implementation  in the software on the access points  they're using  thank you i have another question from  neil regarding  the effective throughput we can expect  to have from wi-fi six he is mentioning  that in traditional wi-fi connection  according to him  uh the effective throughput is  approximately two-thirds of the  of the connection speed and he's  wondering whether wi-fi 6 changes that  pattern  i think it i don't speak for bill but i  think it's going to depend on the  architectural implementation of the  device that you're using as well  that is a large part to do with the  overall performance that you can achieve  versus the bandwidth that's available  via the wi-fi connection  there's so some of the other things that  i've seen like even simple things like  you know the we noticed at one point  with my son's xbox  when he was complaining saying dad the  wi-fi is not working and i took a look  at it and sure enough he was trying to  download a game  and it was only coming down at 20  megabits per second but he had his wi-fi  connection i  checked it on the plume up and he could  see the things connected at 250 mega  seconds  via wi-fi and i'm scratching my head  because the broadband is one gig fiber  connection thankfully these days  and i'm saying what's going wrong here  and after a little bit of investigation  i just through troubleshooting with him  i said are you playing a game at the  moment he says yeah it's such and such  on the background  and he says can you kill that game and  just see what happens so he kills the  game  and sure enough all of a sudden the  download starts ramping up  100 meg 150 meg 200 meg and i said okay  start the game again just to see what  happens  and he starts the game again and sure  enough it falls back down to 20 megs  through push  now one of the things i've noticed now  with the very latest xbox series x is  they actually  highlight that on the screen for you  they say if you leave these games open  this is going to take longer to download  so so some of the  the uh producers or manufacturers of the  products are  becoming aware that their product has a  direct impact on the consumer experience  over the wi-fi infrastructure  and thankfully they're taking a bit of  ownership around that as well  yeah and i just add that you know wi-fi  6 does have  some different modes of operation  but on the whole you know wi-fi as an  industry has this tradition of quoting  speeds by what we call  the physical layer data rate and that  does not  factor for all the overhead associated  with some of the protocols that have to  be running and  protocols associated with uh ip  protocols themselves  and so i think that the the basic notion  you have that is about two-thirds  of the quoted physical layer data rate  will be pretty consistent for wi-fi six  as well  okay i have a question from france and  and he's wondering  um if wi-fi six  he mentioned six gigahertz atelier  and and frequency uh how did his  attenuation  uh compared to to what uh happens with  uh the different flavor of of previous  wi-fi especially when it comes to you  know going through  concrete wall and and brick wall uh  bill unlike the us uh here in europe we  have very thick walls  and uh we suffer from  that constraint when it comes to uh  wireless frequency  propagation and attenuation so is there  anything different  for wi-fi six uh from that perspective  yeah so you know here in the us we build  our houses out of what could  pretty well be described as paper but  it's true that in other environments  the houses can be built out of much  sturdier materials  and it is an issue uh you know six  gigahertz the higher the frequency the  worse the propagation  the difference between six gigahertz and  five gigahertz is probably not going to  be huge  because there is a uh you know  on a percentage basis it's not that  different we see noticeable difference  between 2.4 and 5 gigahertz and one of  the things that plume has is  in its system is um very uh  sophisticated what we call down steering  because you can have an event  where as someone's moving further and  further away from the access point  the best thing to do is to actually have  them shift down from being connected on  the five gigahertz band  to have them being connected on a 2.4  gigahertz span and we will actually kind  of forcibly  make that client downshift into the 2.4  gigahertz band so it can actually be  connected better  as it moves farther away now the six  gigahertz span has another complication  to it which is that under some  circumstances the allowed transmit power  is less than in the five gigahertz band  and that's a complicated topic i didn't  go into but it has to do with whether  you have  what's called an afc system that looks  for other competing  types of services in the area that are  the primary users of that six gigahertz  span  and that determines whether you can use  the higher level power or the lower  level power  but on the whole yes it will be the case  that six gigahertz  is even a little bit more challenged for  propagating throughout homes  and again it's another reason why i  think you need to make very intelligent  decisions factoring where are the  clients  where are additional access points in  the home how strong are the links in the  back hall between access points and so  forth  whether to use the six gigahertz scan or  not to use it because  it is possible despite it being kind of  this new spectrum and clean  and interference free and so forth uh it  is possible to make the network  run worse using the six gigahertz  spectrum than it would with just the  five gigahertz spectrum  i think also as well vincent some of the  things that we have to take into account  for are things like the new building  regulations as well so increasingly we  see  for example the foil back building  materials that are used in new  homes for uh thermal regulations etc  and they wreak havoc with any of the  wi-fi systems and six gigahertz is not  exempt from that  and i think to that extent it's still  critically important even though you  know  we're very much of a wireless mindset  throughout the home  it's critically important that the the  the aps the gateways themselves  and the extender aps throughout the home  at least have that facility available  that they can take a wired ethernet  connection  for households where it's just simply  not practical to get or have to  propagate through the materials that are  involved  okay thank you very much um  an interesting question at least from my  perspective from thomas  um what about power consumption of wi-fi  6  compared to legacy wi-fi and may i maybe  extend the question  what about the wi-fi 6  including its management carbon  footprint compared to  preview generations  bill do you want to go first or july why  don't you start  sure um i think one of the things that  really drives power consumption across  the wi-fi network  is congestion whenever we get a  congested environment where we get an  awful lot of re-transmission  basically the effort to achieve the same  outcome is significantly greater  so having the extra spectrum that  available to us that wi-fi six can  deliver  and also the extra capacity that can be  achieved on the wi-fi six networks means  transmission times are significantly