ices  could not operate in the six gigahertz  frequency band  and if you put an access point in the  home that uses the six gigahertz band  none of those legacy devices are ever  going to go up into that frequency band  and this is where we get into this  complicated question about am i kind of  wasting my resources up in six gigahertz  or is that actually beneficial to me  particularly  in the early days when there aren't so  many uh six gigahertz capable clients  um but the other thing is that certain  of the  new technologies and capabilities like  ofdma  they similarly only work with wi-fi six  devices a legacy wi-fi five device  cannot participate  in that ofdma style of transmission  and so to the extent that a customer  already has a large number of light  bulbs or something in their house that  are wi-fi five light bulbs  those are not going to get the  efficiency improvement  coming from wi-fi six by putting a wi-fi  six ap in that home  but as uh consumers build a larger  collection of wi-fi six devices in the  home they do get that advantage of ofdma  so it all does interoperate but not all  of the features and benefits are accrued  uh to the legacy devices  uh to be honest bill covered everything  i was going to cover so  so maybe maybe you can address this one  i mean you mentioned in your  introduction  um the fact that operators business  model could be impacted by all the  evolution we're currently seeing  so looking at wi-fi seeks revolution  from the angle  of business model and how to market  services do you think that  wi-fi six opens new opportunity and  require  a change in the business model of  operator to  to leverage uh the yeah the services  enabled by wi-fi six basically  um whatever question but over and above  new services that we can bring to market  and sell particularly higher speed tiers  etc and sell reliably  i think one of the biggest challenges  that operators are facing into now  when i speak with some of the larger  operators 